We analyze Algorithm 2 computation complexity scaling with number the of devices D𝐷D with the following assumptions:
a) number of tokens per device ND=O⁢(1)𝑁𝐷𝑂1\frac{N}{D}=O(1) is constant1footnote 11footnote 1This is oftentimes necessary in practice to avoid overflowing device memory.;
b) G=O⁢(D)𝐺𝑂𝐷G=O(D), S=O⁢(1)𝑆𝑂1S=O(1) and N=O⁢(G⁢S)=O⁢(D)𝑁𝑂𝐺𝑆𝑂𝐷N=O(GS)=O(D);
c) M=O⁢(1)𝑀𝑂1M=O(1), H=O⁢(1)𝐻𝑂1H=O(1);
d) E=O⁢(D)𝐸𝑂𝐷E=O(D); and
e) C=O⁢(2⁢SE)=O⁢(1D),D<Sformulae-sequence𝐶𝑂2𝑆𝐸𝑂1𝐷𝐷𝑆C=O(\frac{2S}{E})=O(\frac{1}{D}),D<S and is a positive integer2footnote 22footnote 2Scaling D>S𝐷𝑆D>S would require different use of fractional expert capacity.
.

In our Machine Translation experiments MoE Transformer models shared a) 1024 Transformer model dimension
b) 8192 Feed Forward and MoE hidden dimension;
c) 16 heads in multi-head attention;
d) 128 attention key and value dimension; and
e) 0.1 input, residual and attention dropout rate.

We used the Adafactor [73] optimizer with
a) factored second-moment estimation;
b) first moment decay β1=0.0subscript𝛽10.0\beta_{1}=0.0;
c) second moment decay β2=0.99subscript𝛽20.99\beta_{2}=0.99 with 1−t−0.81superscript𝑡0.81-t^{-0.8} schedule;
d) update clipping threshold of 1.0; and
e) 1.0 learning rate with square root decay after 10k training steps.
